WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.501
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:03.634
We're here with Richard Kind,
everybody.

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:05.167
And we were talking about it.

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:06.534
You've got a lot of projects
coming up.

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:07.868
It's on Hulu.

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:09.367
People are telling me
it's fantastic.

00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:11.334
-I hear it's hilarious.
Oh, jeez. Look at me.

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:12.968
[ Laughter ]

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:14.767
-This is "History of the World:
Part Two."

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:18.067
It's a Hulu series based on
the Mel Brooks film.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:21.100
-This one is the story of
Jesus,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.868
John, Paul, George, and Jesus.

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:24.400
[ Laughter ]

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.067
And yeah, it's hilarious.

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:28.467
It was hilarious to do.
I hear it's great.

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:29.801
And I spoke to your brother.
-Yeah.

00:00:29.801 --> 00:00:33.434
-He said it's hilarious.
I can't wait to see it.

00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:35.868
-And this is based on,
obviously,

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.133
the very famous Mel Brooks film.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:38.767
Do you know Mel Brooks?

00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.434
Is he someone you've been
lucky enough to know?

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:42.167
-Yeah, I have. There's
a couple of things.

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:43.834
First of all, at
the Kennedy Center Honors,

00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:46.133
I sang "The Inquisition"
from "History of the World,"

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:47.334
so that was thrilling.

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:49.133
And there's Obama up there,

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.934
and I'm performing for
President Obama.

00:00:50.934 --> 00:00:54.634
Then I did "The Producers"
on Broadway,

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:56.234
and then I did it at
the Hollywood Bowl.

00:00:56.234 --> 00:00:59.501
So when Mel Brooks wrote
his autobiography,

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:01.634
there's a chapter about me,

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:04.067
"the very talented but loud
Richard Kind."

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:05.100
-Oh, wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.200
That's a very kind way --
-It's so lovely.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.801
-I would say very talented
and loud.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.234
I wouldn't make it a "but."

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:11.501
I would say these are two
great things.

00:01:11.501 --> 00:01:14.100
-He might have,
and I will take it.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.367
He might have.
-Yeah, okay.

00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:16.434
-I thought I memorized it.

00:01:16.434 --> 00:01:18.467
Hold on, hold on.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:19.667
Listen to this.

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:21.868
-They're listening to
everything you're saying.

00:01:21.868 --> 00:01:23.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:27.067
-But this is important.

00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:29.767
Okay, the other week,
about two weeks ago,

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.534
I have COVID,

00:01:31.534 --> 00:01:33.767
and I told your guys,
so I sent the pictures.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.634
So you'll see.

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:40.234
I was sitting in my room
in three-day-old pajamas,

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:43.400
haven't showered, hand down
the pants like Al Bundy.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.234
[ Laughter ]
Just watching TV.

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:48.133
It's 4:00, it's probably 1:00
out in L.A.,

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.133
and I get a phone call.

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:52.601
"Hey, Richard.
Who do you think this is?"

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:53.667
I don't know.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.534
Then I go, "Who do you think
this is?"

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:56.567
I don't know.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:58.367
We go to FaceTime.

00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:02.434
I get a phone call --
listen to this.

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:08.000
Mel Brooks, Norman Lear,
and Dick Van Dyke.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.300
Mount Rushmore.
-It is Mount Rushmore.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.501
-Are calling me,
and Judd Apatow,

00:02:12.501 --> 00:02:14.767
who is I guess, FDR.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.434
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:19.267
Look at this!
-Look at your face!

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:21.467
-I know. I couldn't believe it.

00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:23.801
I couldn't believe it.
There's Mel Brooks.

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:25.534
-And here, by the way,
this is lovely.

00:02:25.534 --> 00:02:27.133
This is all of them in the room,
and they called you.

00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:28.834
Can I just say,

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:33.300
I am in awe of Dick Van Dyke
and his spryness.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.434
All of them.
-They're all young and fit,

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:36.567
and there's all this food.

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:38.200
I would be like this
the entire time.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.033
They're all sitting up
and talking,

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:41.434
which is why they're
thin and healthy.

00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:43.801
But there's Mel Brooks.

00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:44.968
Norman on his phone,

00:02:44.968 --> 00:02:48.467
because Mel probably was
talking about

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:49.567
"History of the World,"

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.434
or they're gonna do "Producers"
in London

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:52.734
and my name came up.

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:56.901
I know Norman as a friend,
and so he probably said,

00:02:56.901 --> 00:02:58.033
"Let's call him."

00:02:58.033 --> 00:02:59.868
And out of the blue.

00:02:59.868 --> 00:03:02.968
It is --

00:03:02.968 --> 00:03:04.100
I'm gonna --

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.267
Don't listen to this,

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:06.934
'cause I haven't got the words
to describe

00:03:06.934 --> 00:03:12.234
just how honored, touched
that I exist in that world.

00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:15.834
-Those people, and I've been
lucky enough to meet --

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:17.267
and I've ever met Dick Van Dyke.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:20.868
They are so warm and giving,
and they understand the value

00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:23.534
of when they tell you,
"Keep up the good work."

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:24.968
When it comes from them,
my goodness.

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:28.400
-Right, well, I have met
Dick Van Dyke before,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.567
the first time I met him.

00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:33.601
I tried to tell him
the effect he had on me.

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:35.200
He would not let me talk.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.968
He would just say,
"You're so good."

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:38.367
It's like he would stop it.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:40.868
He put up an electric fence.
He wouldn't hear the compliment.

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:43.267
But I've told him since.

00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:45.434
Mel will listen.

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:50.000
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.968
-What a delight to
have you back.

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:54.367
I want you to know,
you're one of my legends,

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:55.667
and it is so lovely
to have you.

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:56.834
-It's so good to see you!

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:57.934
-You guys, Richard Kind!

00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:00.968
"East New York,"
Sunday nights on CBS,

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:02.167
streams on Paramount Plus.

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:03.300
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

